WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 202: The Assyrian Empire: The Iron Wolf of Nineveh and the War Machine

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.480
[Rebecca] Today we confront the most feared military powerhouse of the ancient world, the Assyrian Empire.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:21.120
[Rebecca] Centuries before the legions of Rome marched across Europe, the kings of Nineveh perfected total warfare, siege engineering, and psychological terror.

5
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:30.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Tiglath Palsar III, the Iron War Machine, and the fall of Samaria.

6
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:44.240
[George] The homeland of Assyria was situated along the upper reaches of the Tigris River in northern Mesopotamia, centered around the ancient sacred shrine of Asher and named for its patron national deity, the fierce warrior god Asher.

7
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:55.680
[George] Unlike the fertile, secluded valley of Egypt protected by impassable deserts, Assyria was situated on open, rolling plains completely devoid of natural geographic barriers.

8
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:10.400
[George] For centuries, Assyrian settlements were subjected to relentless, devastating raids by fierce mountain tribes from the Zagros Mountains, nomadic Arameans from the Syrian desert, and aggressive rival kingdoms from Babylon and Mitanni.

9
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:17.760
[George] Trapped in a permanent state of existential peril, the Assyrian people developed an extreme militaristic culture.

10
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.080
[George] They concluded that survival demanded absolute offensive dominance.

11
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.320
[George] To be weak was to be annihilated.

12
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:33.680
[George] Their religious theology taught that the god Asher had placed a divine crown upon their king with a sacred mandate to expand the borders of the empire continuously.

13
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.880
[George] Peace was viewed as a dereliction of divine duty.

14
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:47.360
[George] Early Middle Assyrian law codes reflected this brutal discipline, mandating severe public mutilations, amputations, and executions for the slightest disobedience.

15
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:55.840
[George] Assyrian boys were trained in archery, horsemanship, close quarters dagger combat, and physical endurance from early childhood.

16
00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.760
[George] Every aspect of civic and spiritual life was subordinated to the state war machine.

17
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:14.840
[George] In this furnace of constant survival warfare, the Assyrians forged an unyielding national discipline, transforming an entire society into a lethal instrument of conquest that would become the most dreaded empire of antiquity.

18
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] In 745 BC, a brilliant, iron-willed general seized the throne of Assyria, taking the historic throne name Tiglath Pileser Ii.

19
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:33.000
[George] Known in the Biblical Book of Kings as Poole, Tiglath Pilcer initiated the most revolutionary military reforms in ancient history.

20
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:43.480
[George] Prior to his reign, ancient armies were composed of seasonal peasant militias who could only campaign in the summer after spring planting and had to return home for the autumn harvest.

21
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:54.520
[George] Tiglath Pilcer abolished the seasonal militia, creating the world's very first permanent, year-round professional standing army, the Kisir Sherudi, or Royal Corps.

22
00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:04.280
[George] He recruited and trained hundreds of thousands of professional soldiers who lived in fortified military garrisons, paid regular wages from imperial taxation.

23
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:14.680
[George] He incorporated conquered foreign auxiliaries into specialized units, creating a massive combined arms force of slingers, spearmen, archers and engineers.

24
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:28.120
[George] He standardized weaponry across the empire, equipping every infantryman with mass produced iron tipped spears, iron swords, pointed iron helmets, and laminated iron scale armor that deflected bronze arrows.

25
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:41.480
[George] He revolutionized cavalry warfare by breeding massive Nicene horses capable of carrying armored riders, pairing horse archers with mounted lancers to sweep opposing infantry from open plains with devastating speed.

26
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:53.040
[George] He also established an imperial postal road network with relay stations and fire beacons, allowing royal couriers to carry military orders across hundreds of miles in just a few days.

27
00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:03.040
[George] When opposing kingdoms retreated behind massive stone and mudbrick city walls, the Assyrian war machine unleashed an unprecedented science of siege engineering.

28
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:15.360
[George] Assyrian sappers and engineers constructed monumental wooden siege towers covered in soaked animal hides to extinguish flaming arrows, rolling them on heavy wooden tracks right up to the enemy parapets.

29
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:22.400
[George] Archers stationed in upper wicker turrets swept defenders off the battlements with continuous volleys of iron-tipped arrows.

30
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:31.840
[George] Inside these multitun wooden behemoths hung colossal iron-tipped battering rams that swung back and forth on heavy chains, pounding masonry into powder.

31
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:43.840
[George] Simultaneously, underground miners tunneled beneath stone foundations, shoring the tunnels with heavy timber beams and setting them ablaze with oil to collapse entire sections of defensive walls.

32
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.880
[George] Alongside technical brilliance, the Assyrians perfected the art of deliberate, psychological state terror.

33
00:04:50.880 --> 00:05:11.680
[George] In their stone palace reliefs at Nimrud, Khorsabod and Nineveh, Assyrian kings carved graphic, terrifying depictions of their atrocities, showing captured rebel leaders flayed alive, prisoners impaled on sharp wooden stakes outside city gates, and royal rulers forced to wear the severed heads of their allies as necklaces.

34
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:20.320
[George] Royal inscriptions bragged openly of building towering pyramids of human skulls outside conquered citadels and blinding rebel princes.

35
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] By publicizing these horrifying cruelties, the Assyrians terrified neighboring cities into opening their gates and paying tribute without striking a single blow.

36
00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:43.080
[George] By the mid-8th century before Christ, the divided Hebrew kingdoms of Israel in the north and Judah in the south found themselves directly in the path of the relentless Assyrian westward advance toward the Mediterranean coast.

37
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:53.080
[George] The northern kingdom of Israel, with its wealthy capital at Samaria, was prosperous but spiritually fractured, torn by the worship of Baal and golden calves.

38
00:05:53.080 --> 00:06:11.240
[George] Tiglath Pilser Ii marched into the Levant, forcing King Menahem of Israel to pay an astronomical tribute of 1,000 talents of silver, equivalent to 34 metric tons of precious metal, which Menahem extorted from 50,000 wealthy landowners at fifty shekels of silver each.

39
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] When Menahem died, anti-Assyrian factions seized control of Samaria.

40
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:31.720
[George] King Pekka formed a desperate military alliance with King Razin of Damascus, threatening to march south, besiege Jerusalem, and overthrow King Ahaz of Judah if Ahaz refused to join their anti-Assyrian coalition in the Cyro Ephraimite War.

41
00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:42.680
[George] Terrorized by his neighbors, King Ahaz shook like trees in a violent gale, ignoring the warnings of the court prophet Isaiah to stand firm and trust in God alone.

42
00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:56.280
[George] Ahaz sent messengers to Tiglath Pileser with gold and silver stripped from the royal palace and the temple of Solomon, pleading, I am thy servant and thy son, come up, and save me out of the hand of the king of Syria.

43
00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.840
[George] And out of the hand of the king of Israel.

44
00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:04.760
[George] It was an invitation to a hungry predator that would swallow the region whole and enslave generations.

45
00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:09.640
[George] Tiglath Pilesar Ii answered Ahaz's plea with lightning brutality.

46
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:25.360
[George] The Assyrian army swept down upon Damascus in 732 BC, crushing the Syrian kingdom, killing King Razin, and annexing the northern territories of Israel in Galilee and Gilead, deporting thousands of Hebrews to Mesopotamia.

47
00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:32.080
[George] Hashiah assassinated Pekka and surrendered to Assyria as a puppet king, paying heavy annual tribute.

48
00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.960
[George] But upon Tiglath Pileser's death, Hashiah made a fatal miscalculation.

49
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:44.800
[George] He withheld his annual tribute and dispatched secret diplomatic couriers to Pharaoh Asorkin IV in Egypt seeking military aid.

50
00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:50.000
[George] The new Assyrian monarch, Shalmaneser V, responded with furious speed.

51
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:58.320
[George] In 725 BC, the Assyrian army surrounded the mountain fortress of Samaria, laying a suffocating siege.

52
00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:09.200
[George] For three harrowing years, the starving citizens of Samaria held out behind their high limestone walls, suffering horrific famine as food stores dwindled and grain prices soared.

53
00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.560
[George] Desperate mothers fought over scraps, and defenders dropped from thirst and disease.

54
00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:24.880
[George] Finally, in the winter of 722 BC, Shalmaneser died during the siege, and his ruthless successor, Sargon Ii, breached the city walls.

55
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:36.480
[George] The glorious capital of King Omri and Ahab was burned to ash, its famous ivory palaces pillaged, its sanctuaries desecrated, and its defensive towers leveled to the earth.

56
00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.120
[George] The northern kingdom of Israel had ceased to exist as a sovereign nation forever.

57
00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:52.280
[George] To ensure that conquered nations could never rise in rebellion again, Sargon I I enacted the signature imperial policy of the Assyrian Empire, mass population deportation.

58
00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.760
[George] Sargon recorded the historic triumph on the walls of his royal palace at Khorsabod.

59
00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:00.360
[George] I besieged and conquered Samaria.

60
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.560
[George] Twenty-seven thousand two hundred and ninety people who lived therein I carried away as spoil.

61
00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:16.840
[George] The entire ruling elite, craftsmen, priests and military leaders of the ten northern tribes of Israel, Reuben, Simeon, Dan, Naphtali, Gad, Asher.

62
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:27.240
[George] Issachar, Zebulun, Ephraim, and Manasseh, were bound in heavy iron neck collars and marched over 700 miles eastward across the desert into Mesopotamia.

63
00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:35.960
[George] The Bible records that they were settled in Hala, along the Haber River in Gazan, and in the distant mountain cities of the Medes in modern Iran.

64
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:47.800
[George] Scattered across foreign cultures, intermarrying and gradually losing their Hebrew language and distinct national identity, they vanished into history as the legendary ten lost tribes of Israel.

65
00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:58.040
[George] To repopulate the empty lands of Samaria and keep the agricultural fields cultivated, Sargon brought pagan colonists from Babylon, Kutha, and Hamath.

66
00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:14.280
[George] Plagued by mountain lions, these colonists adopted local worship, intermarrying with the remaining impoverished Hebrew peasants, giving birth to the distinct people known in biblical history as the Samaritans, whose theological rift with Judah endured for centuries.

67
00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:25.480
[George] With the northern kingdom obliterated and Syria absorbed into the empire, the Assyrian war machine turned its predatory eyes toward the solitary surviving Hebrew kingdom, Judah.

68
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:35.120
[George] In 705 BC, egotistical son Sennacherib ascended the throne.

69
00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:49.200
[George] Seeing an opportunity in the imperial transition, King Hezekiah of Judah formed an anti-Assyrian coalition with the Philistine city of Ashkelon and Pharaoh Taharka of Egypt, fortifying the massive broad wall of Jerusalem.

70
00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:55.600
[George] In 701 BC, Sennacherib launched a punitive campaign of terrifying magnitude.

71
00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:02.800
[George] His armies marched through Judah like a swarm of locusts, destroying forty-six fortified walled cities and villages.

72
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:13.360
[George] Sennacherib laid siege to Lakish, Judah's second strongest fortress, constructing a massive stone siege ramp that archaeologists excavated in modern times.

73
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:24.640
[George] Carving the famous Lakish reliefs on the walls of his palace at Nineveh, Sennacherib commemorated his conquest, showing Judean captives led away barefoot with chains through their lips.

74
00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:31.760
[George] Then, marching to the walls of Jerusalem, Sennacherib recorded an immortal boast on his hexagonal clay prism.

75
00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:39.440
[George] As for Hezekiah the Jew who did not submit to my yoke, forty-six of his strong walled cities I besieged and took.

76
00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.960
[George] Himself, like a caged bird, I shut up in Jerusalem, his royal city.

77
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:51.920
[George] The tiny kingdom of Judah stood alone on the precipice of total annihilation, awaiting the final blow.

78
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:10.240
[Rebecca] But behind the stone walls of the holy city, Hezekiah and the prophet Isaiah prepared for a miraculous confrontation that would shake the ancient world.

79
00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.640
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our Assyrian saga with episode 203.

80
00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.120
[Rebecca] Sennacherib at the Gates of Jerusalem.

81
00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:31.800
[Rebecca] The carving of Hezekiah's water tunnel through Bedrock, the taunts of the Assyrian envoy, the angel of death smiting 185,000 invaders in the night, the royal assassination of Sennacherib, and the apocalyptic burning of Nineveh.

82
00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:37.000
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

